+# Architecture principles
+
+For whoever proposes a new dependency or changes how larder's parts connect. Start a service proposal here;
+the volunteers who run larder need to be able to keep it running when its proposer is away.
+
+## Keep the services volunteers operate few
+
+Larder's baseline is one Python web app and one Postgres database, with a worker using that database. Before
+adding a service for volunteers to operate, a proposal must show both:
+
+- What it needs to do that Postgres cannot.
+- Which two volunteers can restore the new service.
+
+Being able to set a service up is not enough: it must be recoverable when one volunteer is unavailable.
+The external card gateway is part of the architecture, and a hosted service that nobody on the team has to run
+is an acceptable option. This principle limits operational burden; it is not a ban on outside services.
+
+## Why this is the starting point
+
+- In 2023, Redis for sessions would have left one volunteer operating a store just for sessions. Sessions went
+  into Postgres instead; [the session decision](adr/0001-keep-sessions-in-the-database.md) owns the alternatives
+  and the requirement that deploys preserve sessions.
+- In March 2025, Redis evicted 300 queued receipt jobs, and only one volunteer could restore it. Jobs moved to
+  Postgres; [the background-job decision](adr/0002-run-background-jobs-on-postgres.md) owns the alternatives
+  and the job durability requirements.
+- In [issue 70](../notes/issue-70.md), Elasticsearch was proposed for pantry item search. The discussion reported
+  that Postgres full-text search on item names reduced the "rice" search from four seconds to 40 ms and found
+  "Rice, brown". The proposer chose that approach, so the proposal did not establish a need for another service.
+
+These cases established the rule recorded above. Future proposals should begin with the unmet capability and
+the people who can restore the service, rather than repeat the argument about adding one.
